## Why this tier exists

A vocab entry exists to prevent two documents naming the same concept
differently, or two different concepts sharing the same name. That is
the only job it does. Write it because a term is genuinely ambiguous
across responsibilities, or because different documents are likely to
describe the same thing in their own words, or because a piece of
project-specific domain vocabulary would otherwise get a generic,
misleading reading — not because the word appeared somewhere and
seemed worth recording.

## What to produce

**`<definition>`** — the one true wording every other document should
converge on. Write it precise enough to settle a naming disagreement
on its own: if two engineers reading only the definition could still
argue about whether something counts as this term, it hasn't done its
job. This is the standing sentence a later document points to, not a
description of what the term is for.

**`<alias>`** (zero or more) — the other names a reviewer or an agent
working from a different document might actually reach for instead of
the term you chose, so the graph stays searchable under all of them
while `<definition>` remains the wording to standardize on. List only
spellings and near-synonyms likely to actually get typed elsewhere; an
alias list padded with unlikely variants is noise, and an empty list
is correct when there's genuinely nothing else anyone would call this.
